<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly">
    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
        <RootNamespace>AvCharts.Demo.Browser</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>AvChartsDemoBrowser</AssemblyName>
        <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
        <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
        <!--
            Trimmed, and the reason is the download rather than the frame rate: measured, the published gallery
            goes from 70.7MB in 639 files to 35.7MB in 171, and every one of those megabytes is fetched before
            the page can draw (GitHub Pages does not compress the wasm, so the browser sees the sizes, not the
            brotli ones). What the trimmer must not touch is the XAML: the theme is loaded by URI at runtime
            (`ChartTheme.Create`, and the gallery's own `StyleInclude`), nothing references it from code, and
            the trimmer cannot tell it is wanted - measured: without the roots below the gallery comes up blank
            with `No precompiled XAML found for avares://AvCharts.Controls/Themes/ChartTheme.axaml`.

            Trimming is also the door to AOT, which the SDK refuses without it ("AOT is not supported without IL
            trimming"). AOT is on, and what turned it on was a measurement that had to be taken twice: the first
            one said it changed nothing a reader feels, and it was wrong - the frame it was measured on spent two
            thirds of itself in a layout pass our own code was asking for eleven times over, and the runtime was
            never the thing in the way. With that loop gone (see the design brief), what is left of the
            difference is the machine code, and it is most of it: placing a hundred thousand values in a frame
            goes from 130 ms to 40, placing the thinning a drag draws from from 40 to 10.5, a layout pass from 31
            to 3, and a twelve-notch wheel sweep of that chart has no frame over 33 ms - where the interpreter
            leaves one frame of 200 ms behind after every gesture, which is the one still drawn on the way back
            to the exact picture.

            What it costs is the download: 35.7MB in 171 files becomes 73MB (74.2MB in 180 files here, the
            difference being the emsdk), and every one of those megabytes is fetched before the page can draw.
            It also makes a publish take thirteen minutes rather than one. `-p:RunAOTCompilation=false` publishes
            it the cheap way, for anyone who wants to look at the gallery without paying either.

            The analysis warnings are quiet rather than off - `IL2026` and its neighbours are exactly what the
            roots answer, and rooting is what the trimmer's own advice would be here.
        -->
        <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
        <!--
            On unless a host says otherwise, which is what `Condition` is doing: `-p:RunAOTCompilation=false`
            publishes the interpreter's version of the gallery.
        -->
        <RunAOTCompilation Condition="'$(RunAOTCompilation)' == ''">true</RunAOTCompilation>
        <!--
            IL2075 is the markup face's own warning: a path is a name read by reflection (`PropertyPath.Step` calls
            `GetType().GetProperty`), which the trimmer cannot follow and which the roots below answer - the same
            reason IL2026 and its neighbours are quiet here.
        -->
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);IL2026;IL2075;IL2104;IL3050;IL3053</NoWarn>
        <!-- Same trade-off as the gallery: CS1591 is the libraries' rule, and a browser head is not API. -->
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Browser" />
        <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Fonts.Inter" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\AvCharts.Demo\AvCharts.Demo.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            The XAML-bearing assemblies, kept whole: see the note above `PublishTrimmed`. The libraries are
            rooted rather than a list of files, because the trimmer's unit is the assembly and their XAML is
            reached by URI rather than by name.
        -->
        <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="AvCharts" />
        <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="AvCharts.Controls" />
        <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="AvChartsDemo" />
        <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="iPaas.Controls" />
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
